() Tj
T*
(4. Summary of Remediated Issues) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(| ID | Vulnerability | Severity | Status |) Tj
T*
(|----|---------------|----------|--------|) Tj
T*
(| V-01 | SQL injection in user and admin authentication queries | High | Fixed |) Tj
T*
(| V-02 | Weak MD5 password hashing with no upgrade path | High | Fixed |) Tj
T*
(| V-03 | Missing CSRF protection on authentication and booking forms | High | Fixed |) Tj
T*
(| V-04 | Insecure session cookies and session fixation exposure | High | Fixed |) Tj
T*
(| V-05 | Stored/reflected XSS via unescaped vehicle and booking data | High | Fixed |) Tj
T*
(| V-06 | Booking workflow lacked server-side validation and sanitization | Medium | Fixed |) Tj
T*
(| V-07 | Hard-coded database credentials and silent connection failures | Medium | Fixed |) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(5. Detailed Findings) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(V-01: SQL Injection in Authentication Queries) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Category: Injection) Tj
T*
(- Original risk: Both login.php and admin/index.php interpolated user-supplied credentials) Tj
T*
(  directly into SQL strings \(for example, SELECT * FROM users WHERE email='$username' AND) Tj
T*
(  password='$password'\), enabling attackers to bypass authentication with crafted input or) Tj
T*
(  enumerate valid accounts.) Tj
T*
(- Remediation: Replaced all authentication queries with parameterised mysqli prepared) Tj
T*
(  statements that bind user input, eliminating string concatenation paths. The admin dashboard) Tj
T*
(  uses the same approach.) Tj
T*
(- Validation: Automated PHP linting succeeded, and manual replay of the payload) Tj
T*
(  test@example.com' OR '1'='1 now returns the generic error message instead of logging in.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(V-02: Weak Password Storage \(MD5 Hashing\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Category: Authentication) Tj
T*
(- Original risk: User and admin passwords were hashed with unsalted MD5. Attackers could crack) Tj
T*
(  or rainbow-table hashes rapidly, especially because the values were also used directly in) Tj
T*
(  SQL comparisons.) Tj
T*
(- Remediation: Registration and password upgrades now use PHP’s password_hash\(\) with) Tj
T*
(  PASSWORD_DEFAULT. Legacy MD5 hashes transparently upgrade to bcrypt on the next successful) Tj
T*
(  login to avoid locking out existing users.) Tj
T*
(- Validation: Verified that new registrations and admin credentials are stored as $2y$ bcrypt) Tj
T*
(  hashes and that legacy MD5 accounts convert after authenticating once.) Tj
T*
() Tj
ET
endstream

endobj
